  "_source": "Automatic recounts: NASS, 'Summary: State Election Canvassing Timeframes and Recount Thresholds', updated October 2024, https://www.nass.org/sites/default/files/reports/summary-canvass-certification-laws-oct2024.pdf. Requested recounts: the same summary's August 2020 edition (the 2024 edition lists automatic recounts only), https://www.nass.org/sites/default/files/surveys/2020-08/summary-canvass-recount-laws-aug2020.pdf, except where an entry's 'statute' names the section the deadline was read from instead (MI, NC, NJ, OH, PA, TX: their deadlines are not stated in either summary). Each entry cites the state statute the summary cites.",
  "_use": "Read by app/recount_rules.py. A race is shown as a certified winner only once no recount can still happen under its state's law. automatic: the margins at which the law orders a recount with no request; a race within one is never shown as a certified winner before the new Congress is sworn in, since the recount's own certification is not read separately. requested: the margins at which a candidate (or voter) may ask for one, and the deadline. A condition is {pct} (margin as a percentage of every vote cast for the office) or {votes}; 'combine': 'any' means within if any condition holds, 'all' only if every one does; an empty list of conditions means any margin. A deadline is {days, after: election|certification, business?}; null means the law sets no fixed deadline (or none was verified), and the race is not shown as a certified winner before the new Congress is sworn in. Where a summary anchors a deadline to a county canvass, it is counted from the state's certification, which comes later, so the wait is never shorter than the law's. Margins are measured against every vote cast for the office, the largest base any of these laws uses, so a margin is never read as wider than the law reads it.",
